class.description:
A channel is identified by its KEY, the node and property it samples, and addressed from outside by its NAME, the property it writes. Those answer different questions, which is why they are two indexes and not one.
A key is an identity, so declaring one twice describes one channel twice and creates no second. A name is what a caller holds, and two channels writing the same property on different objects make the name ambiguous rather than making a second channel.
[codeblock]
var channel := tracks.by_key(key)
if channel >= 0:
	absorb(channels[channel])       # one channel, described again
else:
	channels.append(tracks.declare(key, name))

var found := tracks.by_name("position")
if tracks.is_ambiguous("position"):
	warn_and_answer_anyway(found)
[/codeblock]
Indices count up from zero in declaration order, so the caller holds its channels in a flat row addressed by what this book answers.
